  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 302 PM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nSunday morning, ridging is favored at 500-mb just to the west of the \nforecast area, with its axis running from the Pacific Northwest \nthrough the South-Central Plains. This feature would be supporting a \nsurface high pressure across the Northern Plains. At the same time, \na broad surface low is forecast to be positioned across the Four-\nCorners Region and portions of West Texas. The surface high to the \nnorth and surface low across the Desert Southwest would support \nsoutherly winds meeting northeasterly to easterly winds in Kansas \nSunday. Thunderstorms are possible under this weather pattern. NBM \n24 hr precipitation guidance suggests a 25% chance or better for \ngreater than 0.1 inches of rain across most of the forecast area, \nwith the best chances across East-Central Colorado and West-Central \nKansas (60-80%). Thunderstorms are not yet expected to be severe, as \nLREF guidance shows less than a 30% chance across the entire area \nthat sfc-500mb wind shear will exceed 30 kts. However, GFS and EC \nmodel soundings show wind shear at or nearing 30 kts. If this is the \ncase, some thunderstorms may be allowed to become marginally severe. \nConfidence in severe weather occurring Sunday is around 5%, though \nwould increase if wind shear can continue to improve. Regarding \ntemperatures, highs are forecast a bit cooler in the upper 70s to \nmid 80s, as northeasterly to easterly winds would tend to dominate \nover the southerly winds from the south. \n\nA strengthening upper level low in Central Canada looks to enhance a \n500-mb jet streak across the Northern Plains Monday. This would \nallow the surface high pressure to migrate into the Midwest, and \nsupport southerly winds across the forecast area, helping the broad \nlow to the south. Forecast highs are still in the low to mid 80s, as \nthe southerly winds look to be relatively weak, and some cloud cover \nmay still be in place from Sunday's thunderstorms. Showers remain \npossible Monday, but are less likely than Sunday as convergence \nreduces across the region. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance is in \nsupport of this solution, with most areas at less than a 30% chance \nto receive greater than 0.1 inches of rain (the exception being West-\nCentral Kansas in the 30-40% range). Confidence in severe weather \noccurring is less than 5%, as LREF guidance continues to show less \nthan a 20% chance for greater than 30 kts of wind shear across \nnearly the entire forecast area. \n\nDeterministic GFS and EC model guidance begins to diverge by \nTuesday, specifically regarding a trough off the United States West \nCoast moving inland. Looking at GEFS, EC, and LREF ensembles, \nthe ridge from the Pacific Northwest to the South-Central Plains\nis forecast to remain in place, but flatten out a bit, as the \ntrough off the West Coast remains off shore through the end of \nthe period. However, these ensembles show high amounts of \nspread in 500-mb heights under and to the east of the trough to\nthe west, which would suggest the trough is still capable of \nmoving inland between Tuesday and the end of the forecast \nperiod. About 50-60% of LREF members are in support of the ridge\nremaining in place and the trough remaining off the west coast.\nIf ridging is allowed to remain in place and just to the west \nof the forecast area, similar conditions to Sunday and Monday \nmay remain, with cooler temperatures favored. However, if the \ntrough is allowed to move inland, temperatures may reach back \ninto the 90s, and possibly even the lower 100s along the US-283 \nCorridor. Additionally, relative humidities would have an \nopportunity to drop into the 20s based on LREF 25th percentiles.",
